## 2. Principal User Situations

These are the situations Clarify must serve. Every design decision is tested against them.

### Situation A — "I have a vague worry and no structure"

A person in their 40s or 50s has noticed a parent slowing down, or has had a mildly concerning GP comment, or has read something that unsettled them. They do not know what they do not know. They need a framework to organise their thinking before they can act.

**What Clarify must do:** Give them a clear starting structure. Show them the categories of information that matter. Help them see what they already know and what they do not. Produce something concrete they can take to a GP appointment.

### Situation B — "I am preparing for a specific appointment"

A person has a cardiology or GP appointment in two weeks. They have test results, a medication list, a family history they have never written down. They want to walk in prepared, ask the right questions, and walk out with a clear record of what was said.

**What Clarify must do:** Let them assemble known information quickly. Generate a one- or two-page discussion brief. Provide a structure for recording what the health team says and what was agreed.

### Situation C — "I am helping someone I love who cannot or will not do this alone"

A person is helping a partner, friend, neighbour, adult child or elderly relative. The person they are helping may be present, may have cognitive difficulty, may be reluctant, or may have asked for help. The helper needs to work on someone else's behalf while respecting that person's autonomy and privacy.

**What Clarify must do:** Allow the user to work for "someone I love" without assuming a biological family relationship. Keep the helper's own information separate. Let the user decide what, if anything, is combined or shared. Never require the helper to disclose their own health information to work for someone else.

### Situation D — "Something has changed and I need to update the picture"

A person completed Clarify three months ago. A new test result has arrived, a medication has changed, a symptom has appeared. They want to reopen their work, update it, and produce a revised brief.

**What Clarify must do:** Support save and resume via local storage. Allow any section to be edited. Regenerate outputs from updated information. Not lock the user into a linear flow.

### Situation E — "I want to understand what I do not understand"

A person encounters a term, a test name or a concept in the tool they do not recognise. They want a brief, factual explanation in context. They do not want to be sent to a separate website, a glossary page or a textbook.

**What Clarify must do:** Provide concise, inline explanations that are visible when needed and hidden when not. Every explanation must be sufficient for the user to continue working. No explanation should require visiting another page.

