## 3. Five-Stage Experience Map

The five stages are the user-facing structure. They are presented as a clear progression, but the user may move backwards, skip ahead, or work on stages out of order after the first visit.

### Stage 1 — Who needs clarity?

**Purpose:** Establish who the user is working for, what their relationship is, and set the scope of this session.

**User problem solved:** The user needs to decide, explicitly, whether they are working for themselves, for someone they love, or for both. This decision shapes every subsequent question, every privacy boundary and every output.

**What happens here:**

| Element | Purpose | Required / Conditional | Output it feeds | Unknown answer | Privacy note | Burden risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| "Who are you working for?" — three options: Myself / Someone I love / Both | Sets the entire scope and privacy model for the session | Required | All outputs | Cannot proceed without this choice; the tool explains why and waits | Determines whose data is collected and under what boundaries | Low — one choice, three options |
| If "Someone I love": "What is your relationship to this person?" — free text, with examples (partner, friend, parent, adult child, neighbour, colleague, sibling, carer, other) | Acknowledges the relationship without assuming biology; used in output headers | Required if "Someone I love" or "Both" selected | All outputs referring to the person | User may write "I'd rather not say" — accepted; outputs use "the person I am working for" | Free text stored locally only; never transmitted | Low — one short text field |
| "What is this person's first name or preferred name?" — optional | Personalises outputs; makes the discussion brief feel like it belongs to a real person | Conditional (offered, never required) | Discussion Brief, Take-Forward Pack headers | Outputs use "the person I am working for" or the relationship term | Stored locally; user decides whether it appears on shareable outputs | Very low — explicitly optional |
| "Is this person present and involved in this session?" — Yes / No / Partially | Determines whether the tool addresses the user directly or frames questions as "things to ask or confirm with [name]" | Required if working for someone else | Question framing throughout stages 2–4 | Defaults to "I am gathering information to discuss with them" | None — this is a session-mode flag, not personal data | Low |
| "Have you used Clarify before for this person?" — Yes / No | If yes, offers to load saved data; if no, starts fresh | Required | Save/resume behaviour | Treated as "No" | None | Low |
| Brief orientation text: what Clarify does, what it does not do, what the five stages are, how long it typically takes, that everything stays on this device | Sets expectations; satisfies the requirement that all explanations are within the product; reduces anxiety | Required (shown once, dismissible, accessible from a persistent "About this tool" link) | None directly | N/A | States plainly that data stays on the device; does not claim encryption or security | Low if kept to ~120 words |

**Routes from Stage 1:**

- Myself → Stage 2 (self-directed questions)
- Someone I love → Stage 2 (questions framed for gathering on behalf of another)
- Both → Stage 2 (questions asked once per person, clearly separated; user works through Person A then Person B, or interleaves)

### Stage 2 — What do we know?

**Purpose:** Capture the information the user already has, in their own words, without requiring medical knowledge or precision.

**User problem solved:** Most people have scattered, incomplete, half-remembered information. They have a medication box in the kitchen, a letter from a specialist they read once, a vague sense that "Dad's brother had something with his heart." This stage gives them a structure to write it all down without judgement.

**What happens here:**

The stage is organised into information categories. Each category is a collapsible section. The user fills in what they can and leaves the rest. No category is mandatory.

| Category | Fields / prompts | Required / Conditional | Output it feeds | Unknown answer handling | Who it concerns | Privacy / sharing | Burden risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Basic details** | Age or age range; sex assigned at birth (with "prefer not to say" and "not relevant" options); height and weight (optional, with "I don't know" accepted) | Conditional — age/sex asked because they affect what a health team will want to know; height/weight explicitly optional | Discussion Brief, Heart Risk Audit Record | "I don't know" or "prefer not to say" accepted for every field; the Missing-Information Summary will note the gap | User or person they love (depending on Stage 1) | Stored locally; user chooses whether basic details appear on shareable outputs | Low — three short fields, all with "don't know" options |
| **Current health team** | GP name/practice (optional); any specialists currently involved (optional); next known appointment date (optional) | Conditional — asked because the Discussion Brief is more useful if it names the relevant clinician | Discussion Brief, Next-Action Record | "I don't know" or "I'd rather not include this" accepted; brief uses "my/their health team" generically | User or person they love | Names of clinicians are personal data; stored locally; user decides inclusion in shareable outputs | Low — optional fields |
| **Known conditions and diagnoses** | Free-text area: "What conditions, diagnoses or health problems are already known?" with prompt examples (high blood pressure, diabetes, high cholesterol, previous heart event, arrhythmia, etc.) and a note: "Write what you know in your own words. You do not need to be precise." | Required section (but the content within it is whatever the user can provide, including "none that I know of") | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief, Missing-Information Summary | "None that I know of" is a valid, complete answer; "I'm not sure" triggers a note in the Missing-Information Summary | User or person they love | Health data; stored locally; appears in shareable outputs only if user selects it | Medium — free text can feel demanding; mitigated by examples and the explicit permission to be imprecise |
| **Current medications and supplements** | Free-text area: "What medications, supplements or regular treatments are currently taken?" with prompt: "Check the kitchen, the bathroom cabinet, the repeat prescription list. Approximate names and doses are fine." | Required section (content may be "none") | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief | "None" or "I don't know" accepted; "I don't know" noted as a gap | User or person they love | Health data; local only | Medium — same mitigation as above |
| **Recent test results or measurements** | Free-text area: "Are there any recent test results, scans, blood pressure readings, cholesterol numbers or other measurements you can recall or have to hand?" with prompt: "You do not need to interpret them. Write down what you can see or remember." | Conditional — shown because test results are often the trigger for seeking clarity | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief, Missing-Information Summary | "None" or "I had something done but I can't remember the numbers" — both valid; the latter becomes a gap in the Missing-Information Summary | User or person they love | Health data; local only; user decides inclusion in shareable outputs | Medium |
| **Symptoms or changes noticed** | Free-text area: "What symptoms, changes or worries prompted this session?" with prompt: "This can be physical, emotional, or simply a sense that something is not right. Write it in your own words." | Required section (content may be "I'm not sure — I just want to check") | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief, Missing-Information Summary | "I'm not sure" is valid and explicitly welcomed | User or person they love | Health data; local only | Low — this is often the easiest question because it is the reason they are here |
| **Family and shared history** | Free-text area: "Is there any heart-related or relevant health history in the family or household?" with prompt: "This includes biological family, but also shared living situations, shared environments, or shared lifestyle factors that might be relevant. You do not need to know exact diagnoses — 'something with his heart' is enough." | Required section (content may be "I don't know" or "nothing I'm aware of") | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief, Missing-Information Summary | "I don't know" is valid and becomes a noted gap; "I was adopted" or "I don't have contact with family" accepted without further probing | User or person they love; explicitly includes non-biological shared risk | Health data; family data is sensitive; local only; user decides inclusion | Medium — family history questions can be emotionally loaded; the prompt must be gentle and inclusive |
| **Lifestyle factors the user wishes to note** | Free-text area: "Are there lifestyle factors you think are relevant and want to record?" with prompt examples (smoking, alcohol, activity level, sleep, stress, diet, occupation). Note: "Only include what you want to. This is not a lifestyle audit." | Conditional — offered, never required | Heart Risk Audit Record, Discussion Brief | Skipped entirely if user does not wish to engage; no gap noted (this is optional by design) | User or person they love | Health-adjacent data; local only | Low if genuinely optional and framed as such |
| **Documents to hand** | Checklist: "Do you have any of the following available?" — GP summary / discharge letter / test results / medication list / previous correspondence / none of these / I don't know what I have | Conditional — asked because the Discussion Brief can reference what the user should bring | Discussion Brief, Missing-Information Summary | "I don't know what I have" is valid; becomes a practical action item ("Gather documents before the appointment") | User or person they love | No data captured — just a checklist of what exists | Very low |

**Orientation for Stage 2:** A brief note (~60 words) at the top: "This stage is about writing down what you already know. You do not need to be accurate, complete or medical. Approximate, partial and 'I think' answers are all useful. You can come back and edit anything. Nothing you write here leaves this device."

**Explanations available within Stage 2:** Each category header has a small "Why is this asked?" toggle. When expanded, it shows one to three sentences explaining why a health team would want this information. Example for Family and shared history: "A health team will ask about heart conditions in close relatives because some risks are inherited. They may also ask about shared living conditions or habits. You do not need to know exact diagnoses — approximate information is useful."

**Routes from Stage 2:**

- All categories addressed (even with "I don't know") → Stage 3
- User wishes to skip to Stage 3 with incomplete information → permitted; gaps carry forward
- User working for "Both" → completes Stage 2 for Person A, then Stage 2 for Person B (or interleaves; the tool tracks which person is active)

### Stage 3 — What remains unknown or unclear?

**Purpose:** Transform the gaps, uncertainties and "I don't knows" from Stage 2 into a clear, structured summary. Show the user what they do not know, what they might be able to find out, and what only a health team can clarify.

**User problem solved:** After writing down what they know, users often feel a vague sense of incompleteness but cannot articulate what is missing. This stage makes the gaps visible, categorises them, and distinguishes between "you can find this out yourself" and "this requires a health team conversation."

**What happens here:**

The tool automatically generates a draft Missing-Information Summary from Stage 2 entries. The user reviews, edits, adds and removes items.

| Element | Purpose | Required / Conditional | Output it feeds | Unknown answer | Who it concerns | Privacy / sharing | Burden risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Auto-generated gap list | Every field left blank, answered with "I don't know," or answered with uncertainty language ("I think," "maybe," "something like") is listed as a gap | Required (generated automatically) | Missing-Information Summary, Discussion Brief | N/A — this is derived from Stage 2 | User or person they love | Derived from health data; same privacy treatment | None — automatic |
| Gap categorisation | Each gap is tagged: "You may be able to find this" (e.g., check a medication box, call the GP reception, look at a patient portal) or "Ask the health team" (e.g., interpret a test result, assess inherited risk, recommend further investigation) | Required (auto-suggested, user-editable) | Missing-Information Summary, Discussion Brief | N/A | User or person they love | None additional | Low — the categorisation is suggested, not imposed |
| User-added gaps | Free-text: "Is there anything else you are unsure about, worried about, or feel is missing?" | Conditional | Missing-Information Summary, Discussion Brief | May be left blank | User or person they love | Health-adjacent; local only | Low |
| "Questions I want to ask" | Free-text: "Write down any questions you want to ask a health team member. They do not need to be well-formed. 'Why does my chest feel tight when I walk upstairs?' is a perfectly good question." | Required section (may be empty) | Discussion Brief | May be left blank; the tool notes "No specific questions recorded" | User or person they love | Local only; appears in Discussion Brief if user selects it | Low — the prompt normalises unpolished questions |
| Priority marking | User can mark up to three gaps or questions as "most important to me" | Conditional | Discussion Brief (prioritised items appear first) | None marked is fine | User or person they love | None | Very low |

**Orientation for Stage 3:** ~50 words: "This stage shows you what is missing or unclear, based on what you entered in the previous stage. You can edit, add, remove and prioritise. The goal is not to fill every gap now. The goal is to know what the gaps are, so you can address the ones that matter."

**Routes from Stage 3:**

- Review complete → Stage 4
- User wishes to go back and add information to Stage 2 → permitted; gaps update automatically
- User wishes to skip to outputs → permitted; Stage 4 and 5 can be completed later

### Stage 4 — What needs to be discussed?

**Purpose:** Generate a structured, print-ready Discussion Brief that the user can bring to a health team conversation. Also prepare the user for the conversation itself.

**User problem solved:** People often freeze in clinical appointments. They forget questions. They do not know how to frame what they know. They leave without understanding what was said. This stage produces a document that structures the conversation and a framework for recording what happens.

**What happens here:**

| Element | Purpose | Required / Conditional | Output it feeds | Unknown answer | Who it concerns | Privacy / sharing | Burden risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Auto-generated Discussion Brief | Assembled from Stages 2 and 3: known information, gaps, questions, priorities. Formatted as a one- to two-page document suitable for handing to a clinician or reading alongside them | Required (generated automatically from prior stages) | Health Team Discussion Brief | N/A — derived | User or person they love | This is the primary shareable output; user explicitly chooses to generate it and what to include | None — automatic |
| Brief customisation | User can toggle sections on/off: include/exclude basic details, include/exclude family history, include/exclude lifestyle notes, include/exclude specific gaps, include/exclude specific questions | Required (user must confirm what is included before the brief is finalised) | Health Team Discussion Brief | N/A | User or person they love | This is the privacy control point: the user decides what a health team sees | Low — toggles, not new data entry |
| "How to use this brief" guidance | ~80 words: "You can hand this to the clinician, read it together, or use it as your own prompt. You do not have to hand it over. You can keep it and refer to it. If you hand it over, you can ask for it back or for a copy to be placed in the notes." | Required (shown once, dismissible) | None directly | N/A | User | None | Very low |
| "What to expect" guidance | ~80 words: brief, factual description of what a typical GP or cardiology appointment involves, what the clinician may ask, what the user is entitled to ask, and that they can bring someone with them | Conditional (offered as a toggle: "Would it help to know what to expect?") | None directly | N/A | User | None | Very low if genuinely optional |
| Appointment logistics | "Do you have an appointment arranged?" — Yes (date, who with) / No / I'm not sure. If no: "Would it help to have a prompt to arrange one?" — generates a short script the user can use when calling the practice | Conditional | Next-Action Record | "I'm not sure" accepted | User or person they love | Appointment details are personal; local only | Low |

**Routes from Stage 4:**

- Brief generated and customised → user may export/print now, or continue to Stage 5
- User wishes to export the Discussion Brief and stop → fully supported; this is a valid completion point
- User has no appointment and does not want one yet → Stage 5 captures "what happens next" as intentions rather than actions

### Stage 5 — What happens next?

**Purpose:** Record decisions, actions, follow-up intentions and any outcome from a health team conversation. Produce the final set of take-forward documents.

**User problem solved:** After an appointment (or after deciding what to do), people often forget what was said, what was agreed, and what they meant to do next. This stage captures it while it is fresh, and produces a record the user controls.

**What happens here:**

| Element | Purpose | Required / Conditional | Output it feeds | Unknown answer | Who it concerns | Privacy / sharing | Burden risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| "Have you had the health team conversation yet?" — Yes / No / Not yet | Determines whether this stage records outcomes or intentions | Required | Decision and Next-Action Record | N/A — three clear options | User or person they love | None | Very low |
| If Yes: "What was discussed?" — free text | Captures the substance of the conversation in the user's words | Conditional (offered, not forced) | Decision and Next-Action Record | May be left blank or minimal ("They said it was fine") | User or person they love | Health data; local only; user decides sharing | Low |
| If Yes: "What was decided or recommended?" — free text | Captures decisions, referrals, further tests, medication changes, watchful waiting, etc. | Conditional | Decision and Next-Action Record, Take-Forward Pack | "Nothing was decided" or "I'm not sure what was decided" — both valid; the latter becomes a follow-up prompt ("You may want to contact the practice to clarify") | User or person they love | Health data; local only | Low |
| If Yes: "What are the next steps?" — free text with prompt: "Further tests? A referral? A medication change? A follow-up appointment? Watchful waiting? Write what you understand." | Captures forward actions | Conditional | Decision and Next-Action Record, Take-Forward Pack | "I don't know what happens next" — valid; becomes a noted follow-up | User or person they love | Health data; local only | Low |
| If No / Not yet: "What do you intend to do?" — free text | Captures intentions: arrange an appointment, gather documents, talk to the person they love, wait and see, etc. | Conditional | Decision and Next-Action Record | May be left blank | User or person they love | Local only | Low |
| "Is there anything you want to remember or note for yourself?" — free text | A private note that appears only in the user's personal record, never in shareable outputs | Conditional | Heart Risk Audit Record (private section only) | May be left blank | User | Explicitly private; never included in shareable outputs by design | Very low |
| People You Love follow-up | If the user was working for someone they love: "Does [name/this person] need to do anything? Do you need to tell them anything? Is there a follow-up conversation you want to have with them?" — free text | Conditional (shown only if working for someone else) | People You Love Follow-up record | May be left blank | The person they love; the user's intentions toward them | This is the user's private record of their intentions; not shared with the person they love unless the user chooses to | Low |
| Output selection and export | User selects which documents to generate and export. Checkboxes for each output. Preview before export. Print or save as PDF | Required (this is the culmination) | All take-forward outputs | N/A | User or person they love | User explicitly selects what to export; nothing is exported by default | Low — checkboxes and a button |

**Routes from Stage 5:**

- All desired outputs generated → completion screen with summary of what was produced and how to reopen the session
- User wishes to return to any earlier stage → permitted at all times via persistent stage navigation
- User closes the browser → data persists in local storage; reopening the URL offers to resume

