## 11. Recommended Build Architecture

### Principle

The simplest architecture that reliably supports: branching/conditional routes, save/resume via local storage, information reuse, selectable outputs, print/PDF, accessible responsive design, and later reuse across Navigate and Prevent.

### Recommendation

**Static single-page application. No server. No build-step complexity beyond a single bundler.**

| Layer | Choice | Justification |
|---|---|---|
| **Framework** | **Svelte 5** (compiled, no virtual DOM, minimal runtime) | Svelte compiles components to small, fast vanilla JS. It has the smallest learning curve of any component framework. It handles reactivity, conditional rendering and two-way binding without boilerplate. It is not chosen because it is fashionable; it is chosen because it produces the least code for a form-heavy, branching, state-driven application. The compiled output is plain JS with no runtime framework overhead. |
| **Routing** | **svelte-spa-router** or a simple hash-based router | The application has five stages with conditional sub-routes. A lightweight SPA router handles this without server-side routing. Hash-based routing works on static hosting without server configuration. |
| **State management** | **Svelte stores** (built-in) | The application state is a single session object with nested person, health information and working notes. Svelte's built-in writable/derived stores handle this without an external state library. |
| **Local persistence** | **IndexedDB via idb-keyval** (a 600-byte promise-based wrapper) | IndexedDB is the browser's structured local database. idb-keyval provides a simple get/set interface without the complexity of the raw IndexedDB API. The entire session object is stored as a single keyed value. No ORM, no schema migration tool. |
| **Styling** | **Plain CSS with CSS custom properties and a small utility set** | No CSS framework. The application has ~15 distinct view states and 6 output templates. Plain CSS with custom properties for spacing, type scale and colour is sufficient and avoids framework overhead. Print styles are a separate stylesheet loaded only for print media. |
| **Print/PDF** | **Print CSS + browser print-to-PDF** | Each output is a dedicated HTML view. A print stylesheet hides all non-content elements and formats for A4. The user prints or saves as PDF via the browser. No server-side PDF generation. No third-party PDF library. |
| **Build tool** | **Vite** | Fast, minimal configuration, native Svelte support. Produces static files (HTML, CSS, JS) that can be hosted anywhere. |
| **Hosting** | **Static file hosting** (any CDN or static host: Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Pages, or a simple Nginx/Apache directory) | The application is entirely client-side. No server runtime is needed. The URL heartfirst.shyntesy.com/clarify points to a directory of static files. |
| **Testing** | **Vitest** (unit) + **Playwright** (end-to-end accessibility and flow tests) | Vitest integrates with Vite. Playwright tests the full user flow including keyboard navigation and screen reader compatibility. |
| **Accessibility** | **svelte-accessibility primitives** (native HTML elements, ARIA where needed) + **axe-core** in CI | Svelte's component model encourages semantic HTML. ARIA is added only where native HTML is insufficient. axe-core runs in the Playwright test suite. |

### What this architecture deliberately avoids

- No React, Next.js, Vue, Angular or other large framework. The application does not need a virtual DOM, a component marketplace, or a plugin ecosystem. It needs forms, branching, local storage and print CSS.
- No server-side rendering. There is no server.
- No database server. IndexedDB is sufficient.
- No authentication service. There are no accounts.
- No CSS framework (Tailwind, Bootstrap, etc.). The design is custom and the surface area is small.
- No state management library (Redux, Zustand, Pinia, etc.). Svelte stores are sufficient for a single-session object.
- No PDF generation library (jsPDF, Puppeteer, etc.). Browser print-to-PDF is sufficient and avoids dependency risk.

### Reuse across Navigate and Prevent

The architecture supports reuse in three ways:

1. **Shared component library.** Form fields, toggles, explanation panels, output templates, print styles and accessibility primitives are built as Svelte components in a shared package. Navigate and Prevent import the same components.
2. **Shared information model.** The Person and HealthInformation entities are designed to be reusable. Navigate and Prevent can extend them with additional fields without restructuring.
3. **Shared output engine.** The output generation logic (assemble data → apply template → render print view) is a shared module. Each product defines its own output templates.

This reuse is achieved through a monorepo with shared packages, not through a framework feature. The monorepo structure:

```
heartfirst/
  packages/
    shared-components/    (Svelte components)
    shared-models/        (TypeScript types for Person, HealthInformation, etc.)
    shared-outputs/       (output rendering engine)
    shared-styles/        (CSS custom properties, print base)
  apps/
    clarify/              (this product)
    navigate/             (future)
    prevent/              (future)
```

### Payment gate

The application at heartfirst.shyntesy.com/clarify is behind a simple access gate. The sales page at shyntesy.com/clarify handles payment (via whatever mechanism the founder chooses: Stripe, LemonSqueezy, Gumroad, etc.). After payment, the user receives a URL with a one-time or persistent access token (e.g., heartfirst.shyntesy.com/clarify?access=XXXXX). The application checks for the token in the URL or in localStorage. This is a simple gate, not a security system. It prevents casual access but is not a DRM system. The token check is a few lines of JavaScript. No server-side validation is required in v1 (the token can be validated client-side against a stored value, or the page can simply be unlisted and the URL shared only with purchasers).

**Founder decision required:** The exact payment mechanism and access-gate implementation. See Section 12.

